This module consists of learning the advanced techniques, musical competences and knowledge required to perform a musically demanding repertoire, professionally on a particular instrument.
At the end of the module the learner will have acquired the responsibility and autonomy to:

a) play more advanced and complex notation including syncopated patterns, irregular time-signatures (ex: 5/4, 7/4), irregular groupings (sextuplet, quintuplet) and ornamentation (ex: mordent trill);
b) play technical exercises at a moderate tempo securely;
c) perform a musical work with adequate expression and stylistic interpretation;
d) execute music with an adequate sense of tone, intonation, flexibility and projection;
e) perform from memory selected pieces of music;
f) perform other repertoire pieces of the same level. At the end of the module, the learner will be able to:

a) read and play notation relevant to this level;
b) follow musical markings as indicated in the score (including tempo, dynamics and articulation: molto allegro, larghetto and andantino, martellato;
c) demonstrate a good understanding of how to adjust issues related to tone and intonation during performance e.g. adjusting the tension of the bow;
d) demonstrate a good understanding of the score structure and take the initiative to experiment with different interpretations e.g.  different tempi;
e) prepare, present and faithfully render the music score with intensity keeping in mind the composer’s intentions; 
f) practise and perform with the help of technological aids;
g) follow the educator’s instructions autonomously.  This will be verified and thereby assessed on the journal once the student returns for the lesson with the prepared homework;
h) be responsible for the preparation of the required study work in good time for each lesson and/or performance.  A record of the level of this prepared work will be reflected on the mark given on the journal;
i) follow a study routine based on warm-up, scales studies, technical studies and core repertoire material.   The student will be asked to play the exercises during the lessons and will be marked accordingly in the journal. At the end of the module the learner will have been exposed to: 

a) all key-signatures (5 sharps and 5 flats) and various irregular time-signatures (ex:  5/4, 7/4, and mixture of time signatures in the same piece);
b) advanced ornamentation (e.g. mordent, trill);
c) sight reading relating to this level;
d) music from selected styles and periods;
e) unpredictable performance situations e.g. change of environmental parameters;
f) methods of adjusting intonation whilst performing (e.g.  adjusting length of instrument - brass and wind instrumentalists) 
g) various techniques on how to achieve different textures in tone quality (ex: for piano - use of wrists, hands, arms);
h) being familiar with the context of the material being tackled;
i) correct posture and a reliable technique; 
j) a variety of dynamics (e.g. grading crescendos/diminuendos over longer phrases)  and other interpretative elements including phrasing and articulation;
k) performing solo in various professional performances;
l) relating theoretical elements to practice.

This Award explores how, in a constantly changing World, organisations need to constantly look at ways in which they can develop and innovate. The module will explore how people who aspire to become entrepreneurs, creators and leaders of business ventures have a ‘can do’ entrepreneurial mind-set. The module will also expose learners to the skills needed to lead innovation whilst being embedded in core business management fundamentals.
Having regard to the expectations of study at MQF Level 4, at the end of the module the successful learner will have been exposed to the following: 
•	the nature of enterprise, entrepreneurship and business innovation;
•	how, when, where and how business ideas can be generated;
•	how to develop rudimental business ideas;
•	how to translate business ideas into a business plan;
•	the structure and purpose of a business plan;
•	the possible sources of funding to be able to initiate a business idea;
•	the basic elements of market research and marketing; and, 
•	the theory and models of business growth and innovation. Having regard to the expectations of study at MQF Level 4, at the end of the module the successful learner will have acquired the following skills: 

•	an ability and confidence to discuss entrepreneurship in business and the ways in which entrepreneurship and innovation can occur; 
•	an ability to demonstrate a knowledge of the ways in which a business might be developed, including possible sources of finance;
•	an ability to examine alternative business models and appreciate how to establish performance targets so that progression towards success can be effectively measured;
•	an ability to have an appreciation that establishing and running a new small business may not be suited to everyone and the skills sets needed to make an innovative venture a success;
•	an ability to have an understanding of entrepreneurship and how it comes about;
•	an ability to understand the extent to which specific skills and attitudes may characterise the development of successful new businesses;
•	an ability to evaluate the viability of a business idea and an initial business plan;
•	an ability to appraise the relevance of their market research and initial marketing plan;
•	an ability to evaluate critical factors in an environmental scanning exercise;
•	an ability to assess if starting a small business could be a viable proposition and how to establish possible performance goals; and,
•	an ability to have an understanding as to why businesses may fail and the performance indicators that might be used to assess performance.

The BSc in Accountancy and Finance is a degree course which focuses on all the fundamental principles governing accounts and finance. Must complete the following modules - (a)	The Business Environment (MQF 5, 12 ECTS)
(b)	Economics and Financial Markets (MQF 5, 12 ECTS)
(c)	Fundamentals of Management Accounting (MQF 5, 12 ECTS)
Ensure understanding of the types and purposes of businesses in existence; 
Monitor the interactions between businesses, key stakeholders and the external environment, including environmental, social, demographical and technological changes;
Ensure an understanding of key macroeconomic themes and their relevance to the business world;
Solve economic problems and implement strategic decision making at an economic policy level; Apply cost accounting techniques to assist informative decision-making;
Support organisational management in planning, controlling and monitoring performance in a variety of business contexts; Explain in detail how organisations differ from one another;
Critically discuss how the different stakeholders interact with one another and identify why there might be conflicts;
Apply corporate law and other relevant laws and regulations which govern business organisations to work effectively; Acquire the ability to clearly illustrate the specific functions of monetary and fiscal policy in the economy and recognise how these mechanisms stabilise the economy;
Calculate inflation by means of the different price indices used in economic theory and practice;  Measure and calculate costs within their respective elements, types and behaviours;  Account for material, labour and overhead costs using the applicable cost accounting methods; Describe in detail the role of organisations and businesses and the primary reason why they are formed;
Identify different types of business organisations and the common features and differences between them, such as commercial, not-for-profit, public sector, non-governmental organisations and cooperatives; Critically discuss the difference between macroeconomics and microeconomics and identify the key participants in the market economy;
Identify and critically reflect on the types of fiscal policy the government may introduce, including the fiscal policy multipliers and the limitations of fiscal policy to address national debt; Name the users of accounting information and describe differences between financial and management accounting, with an emphasis on the managerial processes of planning, decision-making and control;
Distinguish and critically reflect on the differences between costs and their elements (such as direct and indirect, period and product, relevant and irrelevant, avoidable and unavoidable and sunk costs), types (such as cost objects, cost units and cost centres) and behaviour (such as variable, fixed stepped fixed and mixed);

