This module deals with the management of health & safety on construction sites as well as the management of waste generated during construction, from the site manager’s point of view.
In Malta these two issues have become not only socially sensitive but are now regulated by law and construction site managers need to be fully aware of these requirements and able to manage situations that are likely to occur every day on any construction site.
This module will provide students with knowledge of the underlying 
concepts and the principles required to effectively manage the health, safety and environment in the construction projects.
Learning outcomes:
•	Identify the various legal and other requirements for occupational health & safety and environmental management on construction sites, as well as the responsibilities at law for these activities;
•	Explain the environmental management principles and legal requirements as applicable to construction works;
•	Identify and describe risk management and risk assessment processes;
•	Analyse steps of health and safety management as applicable to construction site such as PPE, site inspections, accident investigation and reporting etc. At the end of the module/unit the learner will have acquired the following skills: 
•	Identify health & safety risks on site and recommend effective mitigation measures;
•	Apply the methodologies related to environmental management needed to comply with environmental management obligations;
•	Apply relevant occupational health and safety regulations required to comply with health and safety law in the running of a construction site;
•	Be able to support and assist the Health and Safety Officer on site.

In this module learners will understand that every individual child has a right to a safe, secure and inclusive ECEC education (UN, 1989). Part of providing a high-quality ECEC programme is the management of health & safety, safeguarding and inclusive practice (OECD, 2019). An inclusive education is defined as “the process of increasing participation and decreasing exclusion from the culture, curriculum and community of mainstream schools” (Booth, 2000). Therefore, this encourages students to understand the importance and roles of these tenets. In conjunction with the recommended national policies and standards develops learners’ understanding of the health/safety, safeguarding and inclusive responsibilities that coexist within an ECEC provision.  

ECEC service providers are responsible for ensuring all standards, policies and procedures are complied with, therefore learners will develop their leadership and managerial skills, so as to work alongside the ECEC team. The collective goal is to develop appropriate health & safety, safeguarding and inclusive procedures, systems and practises. 

This module address:
A)	Health & Safety Management;
B)	Safeguarding (Child Protection);
C)	Inclusive Practice;
D)	Collaborative Culture.
·	Describe the differences between; procedures, systems and practices terminology within ECEC context;
·	Write a coherent, accessible manual of all policies and procedures as a reference point within provision (easily available for all staff/parents);
·	Write a whistleblowing protocol within ECEC protocol; 
·	Identify the responsibility of ECEC provision in view of the UN Rights of the Child (1989) (recognising the child as an active agent, involving in daily care routines);
·	Write the health, safety and welfare of children, families and staff within the organisations philosophy (ethos, mission statement);
·	Identify a community of practice to; collaborate, inform, CPD policies & procedures;
·	Plan collaborative meetings/events with all stakeholders to communicate policies & procedures (changes/improvements to service);
·	Identify ways of maintaining, storing confidential reports, records, logs of incidents; 
·	Identify ways of keeping up-to-date with child’s (staff): demographics, health records, IEP, special requirements (dietary, medical, allergy, learning), ongoing-progress (learning/development/school readiness); and
•	  Match the needs of the centre with out-sourcing specialists’ expertise; Health & Safety, Child Safeguarding, Inclusive Education (Food Handling, First-Aid, General H&S, Manual Handling certification).
•	Identify Health & Safety Management:
•	Define the different perspectives health & safety  (referring to national policy/standards);
•	Recall the Rights of the Child; referring to health & wellbeing (UN, 1989);
•	Identify the roles & responsibility of ECEC provider (risk assessments);
•	Roles & responsibilities of individual ECEC members (self/others); 
•	Identify structural facets of high-quality service regarding health & safety measures; and
•	Identify ways of working in partnership with parents/extended family members (incl. multidisciplinary team).
•	Identify Safeguarding/Child Protection Protocols:
•	Define the different perspectives safeguarding (referring to national policy/standards);
•	Perspectives of; physical & emotional harm, child abuse & neglect, child protection;
•	Recall the Rights of the Child; referring to safe learning environment (UN, 1989);
•	Identify the roles & responsibilities of ECEC provider;
•	Roles & responsibilities of individual ECEC members (self/others); 
•	Identify the process facets of high-quality service regarding safeguarding measures; and
•	Identify ways of working in partnership with parents/extended family members (incl. multidisciplinary team). 
·	 Identify Inclusive Practice;
•	Define the different perspectives of inclusive education(referring to national policy/standards);
•	Perspectives of; discrimination, racism, xenophobia, bullying, exclusion;
•	Identify ways to celebrate; individuality, cultural capital, interests, cultural, race, family backgrounds, previous experiences, beliefs, social norms; 
•	Identify issues related to multiculturalism, language mobility, barriers to ECEC access;
•	Recall the Rights of Child; referring to equality & inclusion (UN, 1989); 
•	Identify the roles & responsibilities of ECEC provider (self & others); 
•	Roles & responsibilities of individual ECEC members; 
•	Describe the importance of confidentiality and networking with external agencies; 
•	Describe how inclusive education contributes towards high-quality service regarding inclusive practice measures; and
•	Identify ways of working in partnership with parents/extended family members (incl. multidisciplinary team).
•	Label, define and describe early years Developmental Disorders (DD) & SEN;
•	Biological, environmental, psychosocial influences to cognitive development;
•	Definition & difficulties of SEN;
•	Barriers to inclusive education;
•	Supporting children/families with DD/SEN;
•	Forming multidisciplinary approach; and
•	Screening, diagnostic systems & early interventions with ECEC provision. •	Demonstrate understanding of health, safety, safeguarding & inclusive policies, procurers & practices;
•	Understand the importance of health & safety management, safeguarding/child-protection protocols and inclusion protocols; 
•	Design an effective ECEC provision health, safety, child-protection, inclusive policies and procedures;
•	Plan different ways to disseminated the ECEC policies & procedures to staff members/parents/stakeholders;
•	Plan health, safety, safeguarding, inclusion risk assessments;
•	Assemble the data collected from risk assessments and implement changes;
•	Use effective strategies in disseminating changes made to health, safety, safeguarding, inclusion protocols;
•	Design a secure, confidential data management system;
•	Demonstrate how a network with external specialities (health, safety, safeguarding) is beneficial to ECEC provision; 
•	Design a safe, healthy and inclusive ECEC programme for all children to thrive in;
•	Demonstrate understanding of the importance of inclusion within ECEC centre;
•	Use different management techniques for the identification of health/safety/safeguarding/inclusion issues;
•	Practice the Rights of the Child underpin all aspects of health, safety, safeguarding, inclusive protocols;
•	Plan the different stages in detecting/screening for SEN;
•	Use and adopt a holistic approach to screening & managing children with SEN;
•	Create ways to support children/families with SEN within a multidisciplinary approach;
•	Arrange the roles & responsibilities of team members when dealing with SEN;
•	Construct a community of practice that support the health & wellbeing of children & families is paramount in ECEC service; and 
•	Plan to respond in emergencies in an appropriate professional manner. •       Comply with the national health and safety, child protection, safeguarding and inclusive policies and frameworks;
•	Comply with the ECEC centre policies/procedures;
•	Create ECEC policies and procedures manual;
•	Advise in the development of all health and safety, child protection, safeguarding and inclusive policies and procedures;
•	Collaborate with all stakeholders and disseminate the centres policies/procedures to team members, parents, families;
•	Deal with issues related to the development and dissemination of ECEC centre policies/procedures;
•	Negotiate different ways to implement changes to;
•	ECEC centre health & safety management;
•	ECEC centre safeguarding & child protection protocols;
•	ECEC centre inclusive practice. 
•	Monitor changes within an ECEC centre that are related to health/safety/safeguarding/inclusion practises;
•	Promote the benefits of health/safety/safeguarding and inclusion policies/procedures & practises;
•	Create a collective culture that supports the physical and psychological wellbeing of all staff, children and families within the ECEC service;
•	Advise different ways to listen to the needs of children, parents and staff in relation to health/safety & wellbeing; 
•	Produce an effective way to review policies/procedures in collaboration with staff/parents & stakeholders;
•	Propose different ways to developing a secure data management system;
•	Guide and inform team members/parents about engaging with the organisation’s policies & procedures;
•	Respond  to any health/safety, safeguarding, discriminative issue; 
•	Manage the SEN of children (IEPs & multidisciplinary team);
•	Create different ways to support children/families with SEN;
•	Represent accountability for the welfare of all children and staff members.

In order to provide maintain safe and infection free care for elderly, one must be aware of how to protect themselves and other stakeholders from any infections and diseases. 
This module will introduce students to common infection control skills which are carried out routinely within all clinical settings.
•	Develop a sound body of theoretical knowledge underpinning a number of practice skills involved in infection control;
•	Review policies, procedures, and tools regarding infection prevention and control;
•	Communicate information about infection types, control and prevention as relevant to all care settings and of the services offered in public health on infection control;
•	Locate and adhere to outbreak management plans in case of a medical emergency. •	Explain the infection control and prevention process according to standard procedures;
•	Describe the procedure to maintain hand hygiene and disinfected envelopment to avoid and prevent infection;
•	Provide and maintain a clean and disinfected caring environment free from hazards;
•	Use of personal protective clothing and equipment (PPE) as applicable;
•	Identify ways to avoid needle stick injuries and how they can be avoided. •	Defining infectious diseases 
•	Types of infections e.g., viral, bacterial, fungal, others
•	The Disease transmission Cycle, and modes of infection transmission;
•	Principles of epidemiology to environmental risk assessment;
•	The Development of a comprehensive infection prevention and control program;
•	Infection control data samples;
•	Management protocols of any outbreak.
•	Basic legal aspects
•	Public services
•	Policies and procedures re infection prevention and control

This module has been specifically formulated to highlight the importance that information plays in the overall running of a facility. Decisions in a facility, irrespective of their importance, are taken based on information and to this end managing information is of an extreme importance to ensure to success and smooth running of these same facilities.
At the end of the module/unit the learner will have acquired the responsibility and autonomy to:

a)	Carry out tasks making correct reference to information which is management effectively within facilities;
b)	Be responsible for decision making which is based on accurate information;
c)	Manage information appropriately;
d)	Create accurate, clear and understandable reports;
e)	Ensure continuous improvement in managing information; At the end of the module/unit the learner will have been exposed to the following: 

a)	Describe the role that information has on facilities management;
-	What is information management;
-	What the role is on the overall success of an organisation;

b)	List different information which requires management in facilities;
-	Financial, supply chain, human resources, procurements, estates, environmental and maintenance, amongst others;

c)	Identify various sources of acquiring and managing information;

d)	Recall uses for effective information management in facilities;
-	Effective decision making;
-	Assess relationships between information, risk, management and change;
-	Risk mitigation;
-	Increase automation; At the end of the module/unit the learner will have acquired the following skills: 

a)	Analyse and manage information effectively;
b)	Report facilities management information in an effective manner;
c)	Use correct methods of data collection to obtain information;
d)	Identify the main areas which facilities managers need to focus on in order to ensure effective managing of facilities through information;

Construction sites demand competent and skilled supervisors and managers.  This is essential for the execution of construction projects in a smooth and efficient manner effectively taking safety and environment into consideration. 

This Undergraduate Certificate in Construction Site Management aims to develop knowledge and understanding of site set-up and management.  The Course covers related construction project processes, legal aspects, management skills, health and safety legislation and environmental and sustainability issues.
The learner will be able to: 
•	 Define the required management, legal and ethical skills, roles and responsibilities of a construction site manager;
•	Identify the different roles and responsibilities of the construction project stakeholders demonstrating understanding of all phases and types of construction projects and the roles of the stakeholders involved;
•	Analyse and describe the impact of legislation and standards on construction site and the principles of compliance and Health and Safety Standards of the site under a construction contract;
•	Compare and contrast he quality and procurement systems, procedures and standards for construction projects; 
•	Analyse and describe the key principles of site set-up and how to ensure a secure and safe environment for the construction works including how to the use recycled and recovered materials;
•	Identify the need for the use of subcontractors on construction projects and the importance of subcontractor management; 
•	Identify tools for managing the quality of all facets of the construction works through architects, designers and engineers;
•	Examine effective communication tools and principles contributing to performance on construction works;
•	Describe an ethical framework in which site managers should operate to manage others including motivational and leadership theories and tools to motivate workers in context of site management; The learner will be able to: 
•	Evaluate how health and safety legislation and other legislation influences working practices in the construction industry and be able to apply this knowledge in site management settings to safeguard construction site health and safety and ensure compliance with applicable rules;
•	Apply  relevant occupational health and safety regulations and methodologies related to environmental management needed to comply with environmental management obligations applicable on construction sites;
•	Assess project managment and procurement processes and procedures in context of construction industry; 
•	Apply the procedures and methods of estimating including pricing and labour projections and collate site data to meet the needs of a variety of estimating methods;
•	Apply any procurement or contractual related provisions applicable to site management complying with applicable contractual provisions and legal aspects;
•	Describe ways of implementing quality management systems, procedures and standards to ensure compliance with contractual and legal requirements, and facilitate the inspection and certification of construction works;
•	Apply concepts and tools related to the management of site setup, site facilities and logistics associated with a construction site;
•	Solve problems, retrieve information and make effective use of construction project, contract and risk management processes and procedures as these apply to constructions sites, including processes for progression and monitoring of the construction works.
•	Apply the management and leadership skills required to manage the site workforce and communicate  effectively in a multi-disciplinary setting and multi-cultural construction organisations.
